      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Two-neuron chain:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " in both divisions, preganglionic neurons in the CNS connect to postganglionic neurons in ganglia outside the CNS, whose fibers run to the effector organs."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sympathetic outflow:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " preganglionic cell bodies in the intermediolateral horn from T1 to L2 or L3; ganglia beside the spine: the sympathetic chain plus prevertebral ganglia (superior cervical, celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric, aorticorenal)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sympathetic targets:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " long fibers to smooth muscle of vessels, viscera, lungs, scalp (piloerectors) and pupils, to the heart, and to sweat, salivary and digestive glands."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Parasympathetic outflow:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " from the brain stem with cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X (vagus), and from the sacral cord at S2 and S3; the vagus carries about 75% of all parasympathetic fibers."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Parasympathetic ganglia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " lie within the effector organs (ciliary, sphenopalatine, otic, pelvic, vagal), with postganglionic fibers only 1 or 2 mm long, so responses are specific and localized."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Overall roles:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " sympathetic is catabolic (fight-or-flight); parasympathetic is anabolic (conserves and restores)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sympathetic effects:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " raises heart rate and contractility, bronchodilates, increases hepatic glycogenolysis and glucose release, basal metabolic rate and muscular strength, makes the palms sweat, and turns down functions not needed for immediate survival such as digestion."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Parasympathetic effects:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " stimulates gastrointestinal secretions and motility (including bowel evacuation), slows heart rate, reduces blood pressure, controls erection."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Transmitters:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " acetylcholine from all preganglionic fibers, all parasympathetic postganglionic fibers and the sympathetic postganglionic fibers to piloerectors and sweat glands; norepinephrine from most sympathetic postganglionic fibers."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Horner syndrome signs:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis and hyperemia on the affected side."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Horner syndrome causes:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " disruption of the cervical sympathetic pathway from the hypothalamus to the eye — central (e.g. brainstem ischemia, syringomyelia, brain or cord tumor) or peripheral (e.g. Pancoast tumor, cervical adenopathy, neck and skull injuries, aortic or carotid dissection, thoracic aortic aneurysm)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Autonomic dysreflexia:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " usually after cord injury above T6 (unlikely below T10): a noxious stimulus below the injury, most often bladder distention (often a blocked catheter), provokes life-threatening hypertension."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Dysreflexia mechanism:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " stimulation below the injury triggers reflex sympathetic vasoconstriction and a rise in BP; the parasympathetic correction cannot travel below the cord lesion, so the hypertension continues."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Dysreflexia signs:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " sudden headache, nausea and vomiting, visual problems, nasal congestion, anxiety; profuse sweating, flushing and piloerection above the injury, dry pale skin below it."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
